  "modules": [
    {
      "id": "uniequip_001_bluep",
      "name": {
        "en": "Blue Poison's Badge",
        "zh": "蓝毒证章",
        "source": "official",
        "isMachineTranslation": false
      },
      "type": "INITIAL",
      "typeName2": "",
      "icon": "original",
      "description": {
        "en": "Operator Blue Poison has demonstrated a remarkable aptitude for maintaining distance whilst conducting suppressive fire against the enemy.\nThe Field Operations Department has thus passed the following resolution:\nThis operator shall be appointed a Sniper Operator during field operations to exercise Marksman responsibilities.\nIn witness whereof,\nThis badge is hereby conferred upon the above named.",
        "zh": "干员蓝毒擅长保持距离对敌人进行火力压制\n根据外勤部门决议\n在外勤任务中划分为狙击干员，行使速射手职责\n特别颁发此证章\n以兹证明",
        "source": "official",
        "isMachineTranslation": false
      },
      "servers": [
        "cn",
        "global"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "uniequip_002_bluep",
      "name": {
        "en": "Standard Color Chart",
        "zh": "标准比色卡",
        "source": "official",
        "isMachineTranslation": false
      },
      "type": "ADVANCED",
      "typeName2": "Y",
      "icon": "uniequip_002_bluep",
      "description": {
        "en": "The dessert craze at the Rhodes Island cafeteria has gone on for close to a week now. These past few days, dinner hours have ended with numerous operators unwilling to leave, sitting at already cleared-out tables looking around in anticipation of that day's confections.\nAn operator who wishes to remain unnamed (and is not a Lateran) confesses to us: 'I didn't believe them at first, when they said off-menu desserts kept showing up in the canteen recently. Then last night, my very idea of desserts got completely upended.'\nWe only half-believed it ourselves when we squeezed into the dessert section and pulled out what looked like an extremely ordinary 'lemon' from amidst the cakes. Not normally eaten whole, we cut the fruit open and got the familiar lemony scent, and even saw its glistening juices flow out from the cut. However, once we tasted the tart-yet-sweet, faintly milk-flavored layers of cake that made up the 'pulp' inside, like the other operators we were blown away by the chef's ingenuity and maestro-level craft.\nWe attempted to interview the few operators that typically make their way in and out of the cafeteria kitchen, but all of them smiled and refused to answer our questions. In the end, with Operator Ceobe serving as an avenue for our breakthrough, we finally obtained enough leads to discover the maker... no, the artist of these desserts.\nWe found the operator in question at the Medical Department. She was busy at the time, and could not spare us an interview.\nAs Operator Honeyberry (who just so happened to be free) informed us, the chef has recently been busy assisting Medical with developing pharmaceuticals more appealing to children.\nA significant number of child patients are aboard Rhodes Island, and the question of how to improve their compliance in taking medication has always vexed our medics. Many children instinctively make a show of resistance when faced with liquid or tablet doses that they hate the taste of. The vast majority of Medic operators can imagine that the simple addition of sugar wouldn't rid a drug of its strange flavor.\nAs of now, though, Medical has developed several successful formulations with this operator's help. Making use of her precise grasp on flavor and color, she's not only been able to conceal the uniquely offputting taste of these drugs, but has taken full advantage of the opportunity, using these medical flavors as a base to create aromas that make your mouth water. Following strict safety tests, these preparations have been provided to the children to immediately fantastic results. According to the little patients, taking medicine on time has suddenly become something to look forward to.\n(Underneath this news article is written in small font: She didn't want us to publish this draft out of shyness, and used more desserts to bribe us to that end... I mean, we couldn't not listen to her!)",
        "zh": "罗德岛食堂的甜品热潮已经持续了将近一个星期。这些天里，晚餐时间过后，许多干员都不愿离开食堂，而是会坐在已经撤空的桌前翘首等待当天的甜食。\n一位不愿透露姓名的干员（并非拉特兰人）告诉我们：“听其他人说这几天食堂里偶尔会出现不同寻常的甜品，我一开始还不信。直到昨天晚上，我对甜品的认知被彻底颠覆了。”\n我们半信半疑地从甜品区拥挤的蛋糕中间拿取了一颗看起来非常普通的“柠檬”。切开这种一般不直接食用的水果，我们闻到了熟悉的柠檬香气，同时也看到了汁水四溢的晶莹切面。然而，在品尝过它内部酸甜可口、还带着淡淡奶香与蛋糕夹层的“果肉”之后，与其他干员一样，我们也被厨师的巧思与高超手艺折服了。\n我们试图采访经常出入食堂后厨的几位干员，但他们都笑着拒绝回答我们的问题。最后，以刻俄柏干员为突破口，我们终于获得了足够的线索，找到了甜品的制作……不，创作者。\n我们在医疗部找到了这位干员。当时她正在忙碌，无暇接受我们的采访。\n据恰好空闲的蜜莓干员透露，这位干员最近正忙于协助医疗部开发更适合儿童服用的药品制剂。\n罗德岛上的儿童患者为数不少，而如何提升这些低龄患者的服药依从性一直是困扰医疗干员的难题。许多孩子在面对气味讨厌的药水和分散片时都会本能地表现出抗拒。大多数医疗干员能想到的只有在里面加糖，药品本身散发出的怪味并不能得到解决。\n现在，在这位干员的帮助下，医疗部已经开发了数种成功的药品制剂。利用自己对味道和颜色的精准把控，这位干员不仅能掩盖药品中令人不悦的特殊味道，甚至还能量体裁衣，在药品独特气味的基础上搭配出令人垂涎的香气。经过严谨的安全性测试之后，这些制剂被投放给孩子们，随即大受欢迎。对这些小患者而言，按时服药一下子变成了一件值得期待的事。\n（新闻稿的下面写了一行小字：她因为害羞不想让这篇稿子发出去，还用更多的甜品贿赂我们……这不是只能听她的了嘛！）",
        "source": "official",
        "isMachineTranslation": false
      },
      "servers": [
        "cn",
        "global"
      ],
